# 🎓 BSc (Hons) Mathematics & Physics – Open University

This page outlines my academic journey through the Open University, culminating in a **2:1 degree in Mathematics & Physics**, awarded on **31 July 2021**. The degree formed a key part of my analytical foundation, reinforcing my problem-solving abilities and developing a rigorous approach to logic, data, and scientific reasoning.

---

## 🧠 Focus Areas

While the degree covered a wide range of mathematical and physical disciplines, I tailored my studies toward the following:

- 🧲 **Quantum Mechanics (SM358)**  
  Explored quantum states, uncertainty principles, tunnelling, and the mathematical underpinnings of quantum theory.

- 🔢 **Complex Numbers and Group Theory (M337)**  
  Developed fluency in complex analysis, transformations, and algebraic structures critical to both physics and coding logic.

- ⚡ **Electromagnetism (SMT359)**  
  Covered electromagnetic theory, Maxwell's equations, and their applications in theoretical and practical systems.

---

## 📅 Timeline & Commitment

I undertook 120 credits per year for my BSc, balancing what was effectively full-time study alongside full-time work. This required a high level of self-discipline, time management, and long-term focus.

My studies spanned approximately five years, with final modules completed under the added pressure of pandemic-related disruptions.

In my third year, I deferred temporarily to explore a potential career path in teaching, an area I found not to be intellectually engaging and ultimately chose to leave. Upon returning, I split my Level 3 modules across two years to better manage the increased difficulty while maintaining my work responsibilities.

This strategic decision allowed me to maintain academic performance while adapting to the demands of higher-level study.

